attacked queen to safety.","Shores up the h2 pawn, which was under as much attack as it had defence (2 against 1).","The next best move, 20…Qg6, would drop Black's winning chances from 53% to 12% (+5.36).","20…Qh7 gets the queen out of the way of gxf5.","White was threatening gxf5, winning the queen and a pawn. The queen steps away.","White answers 21.Rf3."]},{"ply":42,"name":"21…e5","cls":"great","color":"b","headline":"21…e5! The only good move here: every alternative is clearly worse.","points":["Attacks the bishop on d4, which is undefended.","Creates a passed pawn on e5: no enemy pawn can stop it any more.","The next best move, 21…Be7, would drop Black's winning chances from 58% to 29% (+2.37).","21…e5 stops fxe6 and threatens …O-O-O, winning a bishop for a pawn, picking it up with 23…exd4.","White was threatening fxe6, winning a pawn. e5 takes care of it.","It threatens …O-O-O, winning a bishop for a pawn, picking it up with 23…exd4, so White has to spend the next move on it."]},{"ply":44,"name":"22…fxe5","cls":"blunder","color":"b","headline":"22…fxe5 is a blunder: Black's winning chances go from 58% to 16%.","points":["White answers 23.Qxe5+, which takes the pawn on e5.","After Qxe5+, White wins 2 points of material within 4 moves.","Better was 22…O-O-O (−0.88), which castles: the king gets out of the centre and the rook joins the game."],"better":"22…O-O-O stops Bxf6+. After 23.Bd4 Qh3 24.Qf3 Qxf3+ 25.Rxf3 Rh4, the queens come off (−0.88)."},{"ply":51,"name":"26.Rxd7","cls":"brilliant","color":"w","headline":"26.Rxd7!! A real sacrifice: White gives up material and the engine still rates it best.","points":["Puts the rook on d7 where it can be taken.","Black can take, but after 26…Qxd7 27.Qxh8 Qc6+ 28.Kxh2 the engine rates it +5.48.","Takes the bishop on d7.","The rook reaches the seventh rank, where 2 pawns sit and the king is cut off on the back rank.","26.Rxd7 wins a bishop.","Takes the bishop on d7 and wins a bishop once the captures settle."],"why":[{"title":"The idea","text":"26.Rxd7!! offers the rook on d7. Taking it gives White the material back and more."},{"title":"What White gives up","text":"26.Rxd7 puts the rook on d7 where it can be taken by the queen on h7 and the king. That's a whole rook on offer."},{"title":"What it does instead","text":"It takes the bishop on d7."},{"title":"If Black takes the rook","text":"26…Qxd7 is met by 27.Qxh8, taking the rook on h8 and threatening Qxf8+, winning a bishop. Then 28.Kxh2 takes the h2 pawn, and 30.Qf6 attacks the g5 pawn, which is undefended. White ends up a piece ahead: White is winning (+5.22). This is Black's best try."},{"title":"Without the sacrifice","text":"The best move without the sacrifice, 26.f6, is clearly weaker (0.00 against +5.48). Black answers 26…Bc6, pinning the rook on d5 to the king. That would leave White level."}]}],"opening":{"name":"Caro-Kann Defense: Advance Variation, Botvinnik-Carls Defense","eco":"B12","points":["Both sides followed opening theory up to 7…Ng6: 14 moves in all, 7 by White and 7 by Black.","Theory runs out after 7…Ng6: too few games between strong players reached this position to go further."]}}